  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nSlightly drier air is slowly working into the area behind a cold \nfront set up across our eastern counties at 19Z. Lingering light \nrain has all but ended over our far eastern counties. Instead of RH \nvalue sin the 80+ percent range, they are down into the 60-70% \nranges over north central SD west of the MO River this afternoon. \n\nLight winds and lingering moisture overnight will result in fog \npotentially developing, particularly over east central SD. There is \nsome uncertainty, but if fog does develop it will be dense with \nheadlines likely needed for a few counties. The ECMWF ENS visibility \nmeteogram page shows a 30% chance of visibility of 0.2 miles or less \nat Watertown. Northwest of Watertown shows less of a chance of fog \ndeveloping. \n\nAn additional low over northern Alberta Canada will cross into \ncentral Canada and drag a cold front along the ND/SD border \nWednesday morning/early afternoon. This boundary will continue to \nsink southeast across the forecast area Wednesday afternoon/evening. \nThis will bring a temperature change down to slightly below normal \ntemperatures for highs starting Thursday. High temperatures Thursday \nand Friday will be 5 to 8 degrees below normal for this time of \nyear. There is also a  30% chance of showers over central SD \nThursday night. Dry weather will again quickly take hold.There is a \n20-40% chance of light rain Saturday, mainly east of the MO River.",
